# Family Context

> **First run:** copy this template to `skill/family-context.md` (drop the
> `.template` extension) and fill it in. The skill reads `family-context.md`
> for family-specific knowledge that should NOT be in the generic skill.
>
> The file is gitignored by default — your family details stay private even if
> you fork this repo publicly.

## Tree root(s)

Who is the earliest ancestor(s) you've documented? List them with birth/death
years. Example:

- **Heinrich Riverstone (1885–1962)** — paternal line root, immigrated from
  Bremen in 1912.
- **Klara Adler (1890–1971)** — Heinrich's spouse, born Berlin.

## Branches

List the major branches the tree splits into (typically children of the root
ancestor[s]). Use this to compute `branch:` tags on person files. Example:

- **Mary** — eldest, married Thomas Brennan; descendants in Boston area.
- **Friedrich** — middle child, no surviving children.
- **Else** — youngest, two children, moved to California in 1950s.

## Extended / collateral families

Any in-law families that you're also tracking. Example:

- **Brennan** — through Mary's marriage to Thomas (1937).
- **Adler** — Klara's birth family.

## Surname triggers

List the surnames the skill should trigger on (in addition to the generic
genealogy triggers). The skill picks these up automatically when this file is
present.

```
Riverstone, Adler, Brennan
```

## Known discrepancies

Document conflicts you've encountered across sources. Example:

- **Heinrich's birth year** — passport says 1885, ship manifest says 1886.
  Default: 1885 (passport is primary).
- **Klara's maiden name** — sometimes spelled "Adler," sometimes "Adlerstein."
  Family lore says the longer form was Anglicized at Ellis Island.

## Name aliases

Same person, multiple names across sources. Example:

- **Heinrich Riverstone** = Henry Riverstone (Americanized after 1920) = Hank
- **Klara Adler** = Clara Riverstone (married name, all US records)

## DNA research context

If you're doing genetic genealogy, note kit numbers, haplogroups, and ongoing
hypotheses here so the skill can reference them.

## Special tags

Document any non-standard tags you use that the skill should recognize.
Example:

- `dna/ydna` — files where Y-DNA evidence is relevant
- `research/adoption-theory` — branches affected by an open adoption question

## Notes

Anything else the agent should know: how you organize the tree, naming
conventions specific to your family (patronymic systems, Hebrew names, etc.),
or specific research priorities.
